{
  "summary": "Skip orphan missing-parent lookups during transaction announcements if the peer cannot become an orphan resolution candidate. The check early-returns if the peer already announced the orphan or if its announcement queue would exceed capacity, using the transaction's input count as an upper bound for missing parents.",
  "problem": "When a peer announces a wtxid for a transaction currently in the orphanage, the node resolves and deduplicates all parents and queries `AlreadyHaveTx` for each before checking if the candidate can be added. For orphans with thousands of inputs, repeated announcements from peers that are already announcers or at their announcement limit cause redundant parent lookups that stall message processing.",
  "evidence": [
    "Author benchmark on regtest: for an orphan with 2,430 inputs across 12 transactions announced over 300 invs by an exhausted peer, inv processing time drops from 14.5–16.2 ms to 0.19–0.22 ms."
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [],
    "enables": []
  },
  "scope_notes": "Changes orphan candidate evaluation heuristics by approximating missing parents with `vin.size()`, meaning peers near the announcement limit may be rejected as candidates even if some inputs share parents or are already known.",
  "changed_since_previous": "Diff provided; the logic matches the previous description.",
  "card": "Skips missing-parent lookups for orphan wtxid announcements when the announcing peer cannot become an orphan resolution candidate. Currently, nodes resolve and query every parent of an orphan before checking if the announcing peer already announced it or is over its announcement capacity, allowing redundant lookups for transactions with many inputs. The author measures synthetic inv processing times dropping from ~15 ms to ~0.2 ms for 12 large orphans.",
